      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "The role of 5-hydroxytryptamine (5-HT) in sciatica in lumbar disc herniation (LDH) in rats was investigated."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "We evaluated the effects of exogenous 5-HT applied on the nerve root on pain-related behavior, the release of endogenous 5-HT in plasma, and the expression of 5-HT2A receptors in dorsal root ganglion (DRG) in a rat LDH model."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "SUMMARY OF BACKGROUND DATA",
          "content": "In previous studies, 5-HT2A receptor antagonists improved sciatica in patients with LDH and attenuated pain-related behavior induced by nucleus pulposus applied to the nerve root in rats."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Adult female Sprague-Dawley rats were divided into four experimental groups [control group; low-dose (10 microg) 5-HT-group; high-dose (30 microg) 5-HT-group; and autologous nucleus pulposus (NP) and saline group] and each drug was applied to the L5 nerve root. Von Frey tests were used for pain-behavior testing. To assess levels of endogenous 5-HT released in capillaries surrounding inflamed nerve roots, we measured 5-hydroxyindole acetic acid (5-HIAA), a metabolite of 5-HT. Expression of 5-HT2A receptors in the left L5 DRG was examined by immunohistochemical and immunoblotting analyses in the control and NP groups."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Mechanical withdrawal thresholds of the high-dose 5-HT and the NP groups were significantly decreased after surgery compared with the control group and recovered after 14 days in the high-dose 5-HT group. 5-HIAA in plasma was increased by nucleus pulposus applied on the nerve root for 7 days after surgery. The expression of 5-HT2A receptors was enhanced in a time-dependent manner by nucleus pulposus."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Exogenous 5-HT to the nerve root induced pain-related behavior with short-lasting effects compared with the nucleus pulposus application. 5-HIAA content in plasma and expression of 5-HT2A receptors in DRG neurons increased early time points after the nucleus pulposus application. These results suggest that 5-HT plays a role in the early phase of the chemical pathogenesis of sciatica in LDH in rats."
        }
      ]},
